  "sources": [
    {
      "path": "experiments/registry-overrides/adaptive-power-identity-terra-20261102/controller-identities-61742.json",
      "sha256": "771bbe24cbc475c374a63d9dff0e61051d2e8b62c080defb135b1ae6b5e7f70d",
      "findings": [
        "736DB0 uses controller-specific jump table 7370BC, initializes row first four bytes to FF 00 0B 04, then selected cases overwrite byte row+F2, identifying the adaptive object index; 734600 independently maps that same controller index to the recorded registry literal. The row byte identifies a relation, not runtime presence or enabled state.",
        "The names are exactly the source literal abbreviations: do not expand RG, PG or RPG or merge similarly named video controllers without another source join. Adaptive index1 is not identified by this row mapping; no label is inferred.",
        "8F59B0 constructs parent+12C8 object, initializes eleven presence bits, filters according to object and GPU capabilities, and checks feature policy. 8F5980 tests a given index less than 11 against presence bitmap. The first-field threshold handler conditionally associates the selected row with this object. These facts do not prove any specific index is present or expose a user-toggle contract.",
        "NVIDIA-authored older GK20A AP source explicitly calls the corresponding historical system adaptive power, identifies graphics controller0, and describes its algorithm as updating idle filters based on predicted savings and residency. Its older protocol and controller numbering are not source-bound to the current object; this supplies conceptual context only, not present firmware policy or measurable effect.",
        "Reason-bit numeric controls remain unnamed and no setter or readback qualification is asserted in this identity audit. User-facing per-engine labels can reflect mapped source associations only once separate API and readable state are established."
      ],
