    {
      "path": "experiments/registry-overrides/adaptive-power-terra-20261102/adaptive-threshold-host-boundary-61742.json",
      "sha256": "8630818d151499ead3214ddfb74003b7bd178941c07c10d15149ca371274fac5",
      "findings": [
        "8F61F0 type4 attribute10 modifies selected auxiliary reason bit1; an enabled-to-disabled transition calls B9C340, and a transition back to all-reasons-zero calls B9C420. Role1 alone has an additional GPU+2120 virtual+598 call with 1000 on the disable transition; roles0,4,5,6,7 do not take that direct role1 branch.",
        "B9C340 constructs firmware header 00/03/0D with the selected role byte. B9C420 constructs header 02/03/0C with the selected role byte. Neither inspected packet contains an explicit threshold number, and the inspected direct host transition path has no call to the known threshold converter or raw threshold register writer.",
        "B9825E initializes a GR-related parent threshold field to 70000 on one constructor path, but matching that literal to a measured scaled 70000 does not establish that this initialization or default generator caused the adaptive-policy response. The firmware receiver, dynamic policy and timing behavior are not decoded.",
        "User-reported local trial observed selected first threshold 70000 while the second word was unchanged after adaptive transitions; in an earlier trial the GR first threshold later returned to its previous value without numeric write. These observations are scoped to the reported adapter/trial and do not establish a universal timing guarantee or physical latency. Capture exact selected and peer readback per step; no threshold restoration framework is inferred as a prerequisite for the manual policy control."
      ],
